<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <parent>
        <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
        <artifactId>parallel-consumer-parent</artifactId>
        <version>0.6.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>

    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-core</artifactId>
    <name>Parallel Consumer - Core</name>

    <properties>
        <!-- Root of the per-fork test log silos; see the surefire argLine below and docs/logging.md. -->
        <pc.log.dir>target/pc-logs</pc.log.dir>
        <!-- JUnit 5 thread-parallelism for THIS module's tests. Declared once here because surefire and
             failsafe both need it verbatim, and a copy in each drifted the moment one gained a comment.
             A property is the right home: property values preserve newlines, which the JUnit
             configurationParameters format needs.

             The high dynamic.factor is deliberate: most of these tests are io bound, not cpu bound.

             This used to live in src/test/resources/junit-platform.properties, which was a bug: that file
             is packaged at the ROOT of the core tests jar, and eight modules depend on that jar (the four
             integrations plus every example). JUnit reads the first junit-platform.properties on the
             classpath, so all eight silently ran with whatever value ${parallel-tests} happened to hold
             WHEN CORE WAS BUILT - their own setting was never consulted.

             That inverted the intent of the settings: the `ci` profile sets parallel-tests=false on
             purpose, because JUnit thread parallelism contends on static state and CI parallelises by
             forking JVMs instead. A downstream module could nonetheless inherit enabled=true, factor=20
             from a locally-built jar.

             As surefire/failsafe configurationParameters the setting is per-module by construction - each
             module evaluates its own ${parallel-tests}, nothing is packaged, nothing leaks, and these
             outrank a properties file if one is ever reintroduced. -->
        <junit.parallelism.configuration.parameters>
            junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.enabled=${parallel-tests}
            junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.mode.default=concurrent
            junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.config.dynamic.factor=20
        </junit.parallelism.configuration.parameters>
    </properties>

